Record the parser's bracket look-ahead instead of repeating it.

js_parse_skip_parens_token scans from a bracket to its match to tell
an arrow function's parameters from a parenthesized expression, and a
destructuring target from an object or array literal. The parser calls
it at every such bracket, and a scan passes over every bracket inside,
which is scanned again when the parser reaches it: once per level of
nesting. On GitHub's 980 KB code view module the look-ahead was 29% of
the compile time.

The scan now records, in a hash table of the parse state, what a scan
from each bracket of 32 bytes or more that it sees closed would return
(the token after the match, the skip bits, a line break before that
token); a later call at a recorded bracket returns the record. A scan's
result depends only on the text from its bracket on, so the bytecode
is unchanged: the 77 modules of GitHub's repository page, Aura's tests
and QuickJS's tests compile to the same bytes. Compiling the 77 modules
(5.1 MB) took 266 ms instead of 299 ms on the host. The table is freed
when the parse ends (14,000 entries, 256 KB, for the code view module;
at most 65,536 entries).

